#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Linear Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8559e2bf-8b48-5e26-81c6-0db03d93645a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5451" :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060608/assets/0394b5d3a371caf95d5e701c0ffb00894eb685e706d8b70af2149057de0b0cb5.png)" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as depicted below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060608/assets/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060608/assets/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents the reflection across the $x$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "166cd7e4-5568-5769-8bc6-4ffc4ed8b508" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Dilation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/id #uuid "ef17b637-7c4d-5515-99b7-13b1290244f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5452" :question/problem "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060608/assets/bd6574ee05b60919de656a889f8532e5a512b381eae63f7a65f251a7afe72738.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060608/assets/be73c2a933a6fb99eaa389774a618848bdadd9cafdf0fcd2f6af126b49b87707.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060608/assets/4866597a50fbdb530e9275c77f2ca926c932c7cc334635bd05191aaddd510d75.png)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060608/assets/962b9458714f9b7f6b17fe2259e140dfabba443d8ecb5b9e2328172ab3f8a1a2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Linear Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c08b2e-af16-5580-a003-c5315f82167c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection across the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339974} {:answer/id #uuid "54c5443c-d4ad-57c4-a5a5-c0cb545360ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339975} {:answer/id #uuid "1b07150b-9e4a-5808-ac2d-8973d2ef0d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339976} {:answer/id #uuid "92357434-128b-55b5-95bc-27796d8e989f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $y=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339977} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad2d62f-2516-5bae-943d-03608de958b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $x=\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c5443c-d4ad-57c4-a5a5-c0cb545360ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339975} :answer-field/id #uuid "c165f199-9de5-50c9-902a-7db02135c0be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0889336-7439-51e1-b086-9f7afdf6cb92" :question/math-academy-id "q-12041" :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "166cd7e4-5568-5769-8bc6-4ffc4ed8b508" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Dilation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35881203-e684-5ae4-94be-3282e15b583b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339981} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf89fab-6738-5f87-b160-41f9e857fa13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339982} {:answer/id #uuid "70e31f39-5481-5594-b7a7-fdf07cf2add2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339983} {:answer/id #uuid "d94ff6ec-cfa7-5498-afb4-86bed98414d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339984} {:answer/id #uuid "67b1daca-6a95-5e43-bf26-d4b6a5056e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67b1daca-6a95-5e43-bf26-d4b6a5056e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339985} :answer-field/id #uuid "f96a2766-3142-539f-82a7-d4cf1a471588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3874682c-2ffc-5970-853a-42b52eea61b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159760" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{1}{4}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Linear Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96adf9f7-9914-5f95-a5b8-7a17f4c2718d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $y=x+\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339988} {:answer/id #uuid "0a6714bd-b564-53b6-8d3b-4827d25a1f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339989} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc29bbc-b7d2-53f1-959d-1450d2e2e1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339990} {:answer/id #uuid "0d860095-5935-5e66-8538-2ea27020421e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339991} {:answer/id #uuid "da169172-2cbe-5771-81b0-f703b5372fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $y=\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dc29bbc-b7d2-53f1-959d-1450d2e2e1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339990} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3279aa6-11d7-5b56-bb7c-d3d389d48c79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-fi